 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S441-009\", \"clauses\": {\"compare\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F04\", \"template\": \"Which is {comp_word}: {val_a} {unit_a} or {val_b} {unit_b}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F04 is question text that asks which of two values is longer or shorter. The comp_word slot takes longer or shorter, so the question is a comparison of two lengths, and this is the family that best produces the compare clause.\"}, \"lengths\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F03\", \"template\": \"The {comparison} {measure} is {answer} {count_noun}.\", \"reasoning\": \"The measure slot of F03 draws from the two compare measures, one of which is length, so hint text addresses lengths. No question family prints the word length, so F03 is the only family that names lengths.\"}, \"distances\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F02\", \"template\": \"The distance from the bench to the tree is {val_a} {unit_a}. The distance from the gate to the tree is {val_b} {unit_b}. Which distance is {comp_word}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F02 is question text that names distances in both sentences and asks which distance is longer or shorter. It is the family that best produces the distances clause.\"}, \"non_standard_units\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F04\", \"template\": \"Which is {comp_word}: {val_a} {unit_a} or {val_b} {unit_b}?\", \"reasoning\": \"The unit_a and unit_b slots of F04 draw from the non-standard unit list crayons, hands and paperclips, so the question shows non-standard units. Problems where both units are the same are in the domain, so the representation is produced.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F01\", \"F02\", \"F03\", \"F04\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"Compare {val_a} {count_noun} and {val_b} {count_noun2}.\", \"The distance from the bench to the tree is {val_a} {unit_a}. The distance from the gate to the tree is {val_b} {unit_b}. Which distance is {comp_word}?\", \"The {comparison} {measure} is {answer} {count_noun}.\", \"Which is {comp_word}: {val_a} {unit_a} or {val_b} {unit_b}?\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"F01 and F03 draw count_noun from a generator's own choice list that holds both singular and plural unit words (block, blocks, and so on). That slot is derived only from unit, not from the printed value, and the value slots print numbers from 2 to 9. So a problem can print a plural-needing number with a singular noun, which is ungrammatical text for a grade 1 learner. F02 and F04 draw unit_a and unit_b independently from their unit lists (F02 blocks and hands; F04 crayons, hands and paperclips), and distinct_in_every_render is false for both. So one problem can compare a value in one non-standard unit with a value in a different non-standard unit and key a longer or shorter answer. Lengths and distances measured in different units cannot be ranked from the numbers alone, so the keyed answer can be wrong and the problem is misleading for the compare clause. F05 and F06 hold: F05 prints only the numbers 2 to 9 in a fixed order, and F06 prints one option key from A to D. Vocabulary is within the gates: no NOT_YET_KNOWN term appears in any family, and longer, shorter, distance, length and unit words are curriculum words for this lc.\"}",
